  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1215 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nThe next shortwave over the Rockies will shift eastward in\nthe progressive semi-zonal flow aloft. Modest forcing \nfor ascent (strongest passing south of the area) and PWATs\nincreasing to around 0.5 inch will support a widespread \nrain event Saturday night through Sunday morning before it\nexits. Rain amounts should generally be under 0.5 inch \nbased on the PWAT and forcing, with areas south of I-80 \nfavored for the higher amounts where probabilities for \ngreater than 0.5 inch are 30-50% per LREF and GEFS, while\nNBM is 60-80%. \n\nMuch of next week looks to feature semi-zonal to southwest flow\naloft. This will lead toward a warming trend well above \nnormal, as we go through the first week of meteorological \nwinter (CPC probabilities of above normal temperatures at 80-90%\nDec 2-6). The peak of this warming should arrive mid-week as \nthe flow turns southwest ahead of system ejecting from the \ndesert southwest. This system will bring our next chance of \nprecipitation late in the week, and given that southwest systems\ntend to slow I wouldn't be surprised to see PoPs come down more \nfor Thursday and increase on Friday. Given the lack of cold air \nwith this storm system rain and wind would be the primary impacts,\nbut we'd also need to keep an eye on the thunderstorm potential \nas well.",
